// #2760 RC leak guard: a mutable slot whose initializer's spine ends in a
// PROJECTION.
//
// `classify_let_value_heap` calls such an initializer borrowed, so the slot is
// not recorded owning -- and the retain that promotes a slot to owning admitted
// a borrow-returning CALL leaf only. Neither fires, so the allocation assigned
// into the slot next has no owner and is never released.
//
// WHAT THIS FILE GUARDS, stated from a measurement on THIS base rather than
// carried forward. An earlier revision of this header attributed the leak to
// main at `b59d272`; re-measured on `6507c60` that is NOT what main does, and
// the number belongs to a DIFFERENT tree -- the one where #2760's assignment
// fix is applied and this projection arm is not. Attributed by building each
// variant:
//
//   | compiler                        | N=20000     | N=40000     |
//   |---------------------------------|------------:|------------:|
//   | unpatched                       |       268 B |       268 B |
//   | #2760 assignment arm ONLY       | 1,600,348 B | 3,200,348 B |
//   | both arms (this tree)           |       348 B |       348 B |
//
// So this is a red test for a HALF-APPLIED fix, which is the more useful thing
// for it to be: the assignment arm retains a wrapped borrow on the way in, and
// without the projection arm the initializer never made the slot owning, so
// nothing spends that retain. The middle row SCALES with N and the other two do
// not, which is what separates a leak from this lane's constant steady state.
//
// The complete fix costs 80 B against unpatched, constant in N -- one retain,
// not a leak. That is the price of not silently corrupting the read that #2760
// reports (`total(xs[0])` answering 15 instead of 7 on the production default
// lane).
//
// It lives in its own file because two details of the shape are load-bearing,
// and `rc_reclaim_leak_test.vibe`'s house idioms defeat both -- measured, the
// shape written in that file's style reads IDENTICALLY on a fixed and an
// unfixed compiler:
//
//   `h` is a PARAMETER built once outside the loop. Built inside the per-
//   iteration function, its own scope-end drop reclaims the assigned
//   allocation along with it and nothing leaks.
//
//   The per-iteration tail is a SCALAR. A tail CONSTRUCTOR is ADR-0092's
//   in-place reuse candidate, so the dead block is recycled into the returned
//   value instead of being leaked, and the caller then frees it. That one
//   difference measured 1,600,104 B against 208 B on the SAME compiler.
//
// The gate compiles this under VIBE_RC=1 and bounds __heap_ptr growth
// (tests/gates/mid/run.sh). `main` returns 0: every shape here adds 0, so the
// answer cannot drift with the allocation behaviour being measured.
enum Node {
  Leaf(Int);
  Pair(Node, Node)
}

struct Holder {
  node: Node
}

fn total(n: Node) -> Int {
  match n {
    Pair(a, b) => total(a) + total(b),
    Leaf(v) => v
  }
}

fn mk_pair(i: Int) -> Node {
  Pair(Leaf(i), Leaf(i + 1))
}

// The leak: a WRAPPED projection initializer, then an allocation assigned in.
fn via_wrapped_proj(i: Int, h: Holder) -> Int {
  let mut x = {
    let k = 0
    h.node
  }
  x = mk_pair(i)
  total(x) * 0
}

// The control in the OTHER direction. `classify_let_value_heap` retains a BARE
// projection itself, so the spine rule must DECLINE this one; a second retain
// here leaks at the same rate, and this file's bound is what catches it.
fn via_bare_proj(i: Int, h: Holder) -> Int {
  let mut x = h.node
  x = mk_pair(i)
  total(x) * 0
}

// The other unowned leaf kind through the same wrapper: a borrow-returning
// call. This one was already covered by #2750; it stands here so the pair is
// measured together and a future edit cannot fix one by breaking the other.
fn via_wrapped_call(i: Int, xs: Array[Node]) -> Int {
  let mut x = {
    let k = 0
    Array::get(xs, k)
  }
  x = mk_pair(i)
  total(x) * 0
}

// The guard against the retain going too wide: a wrapper ending in an
// allocation made at the site already owns its value, so a retain here would
// leak at the same rate as the missing one above.
fn via_wrapped_alloc(i: Int) -> Int {
  let mut x = mk_pair(0)
  x = {
    let k = i
    mk_pair(k)
  }
  total(x) * 0
}

fn main() -> Int {
  let h = Holder::{
    node: Pair(Leaf(1), Leaf(2))
  }
  let xs = [Pair(Leaf(3), Leaf(4))]
  let mut acc = 0
  let mut i = 0
  while i < 20000 {
    acc = acc + via_wrapped_proj(i, h) + via_bare_proj(i, h) + via_wrapped_call(i, xs) + via_wrapped_alloc(i)
    i = i + 1
  }
  acc
}
